    directoryVisible: true,
    sources: [{ id: "src_gurunanak_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Guru Nanak (Q83322), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-10-30): label 'Guru Nanak', description 'First Sikh Guru and founder of Sikhism', born 1469-04-15, died 1539-09-22 (also 1538 and 1539-05-07 as other values), Korean label present, image 'Mural painting of Guru Nanak from Gurdwara Baba Atal Rai.jpg'. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q83322" }, { id: "src_gurunanak_britannica", kind: "biography", title: "Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Guru Nanak', written by W. H. McLeod (read in full from the public web page). CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE: revered as founder and first Guru of Sikhism; says little about his life survives except through legend, anecdote and the janamsakhi anthologies; the revelatory experience at about thirty comes from a janamsakhi; his travels cannot be positively identified; four of his hymns refer to Babur's attacks so he had returned to the Punjab by 1520; the last years were spent at Kartarpur where 'it must be assumed' disciples gathered and the foundations of the Sikh community were 'presumably' laid; he chose Angad as successor; the year of death is disputed between 1538 and 1539. Scholarly encyclopedia entry by the historian most associated with the critical study of the sources; it hedges most episodes.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Guru-Nanak" }, { id: "src_gurunanak_eos_kartarpur", kind: "institution", title: "Encyclopaedia of Sikhism (Punjabi University, Patiala), entry 'Kartarpur', read in full: the village on the right bank of the Ravi to which the settlement owed its origin, where Guru Nanak spent the last two decades of his life with his family, which became the principal seat of the Sikh faith, where Lehna (later Guru Angad) came for instruction and was named successor, and where he died in 1539; the habitation was later washed away by the river. Reference work written from within the Sikh scholarly tradition.", url: "https://eos.learnpunjabi.org/KARTARPUR.html" }, { id: "src_gurunanak_eos_bala", kind: "institution", title: "Encyclopaedia of Sikhism (Punjabi University, Patiala), entry 'Bala Janam Sakhi', read in the first part: explains that the Bala tradition claims eyewitness authenticity through Bhai Bala, that the earliest extant version is dated 1525 in its own opening but that neither modern theory of its origin accepts its self-description because of its 'inconsistencies and fantasies', and that the claim was challenged in the twentieth century without being wholly demolished. Used to separate legend from scholarship; the later sections of the entry were not read.", url: "https://eos.learnpunjabi.org/BALA%20JANAM%20SAKHI.html" }, { id: "src_gurunanak_webster", kind: "biography", title: "John C. B. Webster, 'Guru Nanak and the Historians', Journal of Sikh and Punjab Studies 30:1, read in full (about 85,000 characters via the Global Institute for Sikh Studies PDF): a historiographical survey that says, apart from his own contributions to the Adi Granth, historians rely on janamsakhis written long after his death and 'hagiographic, and hence very problematic'; compares McLeod, Grewal, Pashaura Singh and Gurinder Singh Mann on what he did at Kartarpur (the sangat, dharamsala, langar, a devotional pattern of regular devotion, teaching and daily labour, and the nomination of a successor), and records Mann's critique of McLeod's scepticism. Independent scholarly overview that exposes disagreement; the primary works it discusses were not opened.", url: "https://www.giss.org/jsps_vol_30/02-webster.pdf" }, { id: "src_gurunanak_kirpalsingh", kind: "biography", title: "Kirpal Singh, 'Janamsakhi Tradition: An Analytical Study' (Singh Brothers, Amritsar, 2004), preface, contents and closing chapter 'Light Merges with the Divine Light' read from the publisher's PDF (the remaining roughly 650,000-character text was searched for langar and Kartarpur only): a former head of Punjab Historical Studies at Punjabi University who defends the janamsakhis as preserving authentic data from the hymns and Bhai Gurdas, argues against critics who question their historical validity, and describes Kartarpur worship, the compilation of the Japu with Lehna and the death at Kartarpur. Traditionalist counter-voice to McLeod's scepticism, so used only to show the dispute and for the Kartarpur description.", url: "https://www.sikhmissionarysociety.org/sms/smspublications/JanamsakhiTraditionAnAnalyticalStudy.pdf" }, { id: "src_gurunanak_wellcome", kind: "archive", title: "Wellcome Collection record hjc7d4tw (reference 27298i), 'Page 101: Guru Nanak attended by his musician and holy man. Gouache drawing', unknown artist, Public Domain Mark in the Wellcome catalogue API, checked for the portrait only; not used for any scoring.", url: "https://wellcomecollection.org/works/hjc7d4tw" }],
