      // [src_gurunanak_britannica, src_gurunanak_eos_kartarpur, src_gurunanak_webster | c.1520-1539 | scholarly encyclopedia + reference work + historiographical survey] McLeod writes that the last years were spent at Kartarpur, where disciples are assumed to have gathered, 'presumably' the period when the foundations of the Sikh community were laid, and that Nanak chose Angad as successor; the Encyclopaedia of Sikhism has him settling at Kartarpur for his last two decades with Lehna coming for instruction and being named successor; Webster reports that McLeod, Pashaura Singh and Mann all see an organised community there (a pattern of devotion, teaching and daily labour), with Mann going furthest in calling him the founder of a panth with its own institutions. McLeod's own wording is hedged and Mann argues McLeod is too sceptical, so the row rests on community-building that is documented in outline but not in detail.
