      proactive_agency: [64, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_mikimoto_nagai, src_mikimoto_gia | 1890-1921 | scholarly review + gemological institute] Nagai explains that natural pearls and Akoya oysters were collapsing from overfishing and that Mikimoto, who dealt in natural pearls, aimed to produce gem-quality pearls and carved out a market in which cultured pearls were treated as jewellery; GIA says his output made pearls affordable. This is a documented business outcome, but it is the authors' reading of his intent.
      opportunity_sensing: [62, 0.48, "s", "A"],
      // [src_mikimoto_nagai, src_mikimoto_maa, src_mikimoto_pbs | 1893-1916 | scholarly review + museum catalogue + popular science article] Nagai says his work with Professor Mitsukuri produced great success and that he signed an agreement with Nishikawa to test the piece method at his Tatoku laboratory; the Cambridge record notes Nishikawa's marriage into the family. PBS, however, says he used his patent position to eclipse Nishikawa and Mise, and Nagai and GIA show the round-pearl method came from them, so the pattern is partnership with scientists combined with strong control of commercialisation.
